pecially multiplayer games but  there was this one that we played  all the time and that was marathon made  by bungie and it was really  fantastic way to you know connect  designers from one studio to another and  just kind of  glued us all together in a way that  excited me enough about  the potential with the gaming industry  bungie was started in 1991 by two  young college students alex serapin and  jason jones and the team chose to focus  solely on making net games  just because it was less crowded than  windows what followed was a couple games  with some modest success like  operation desert storm or pathways to  darkness but  it was marathon that was bungie's big  breakout success  or better known as max answer  to doom at that time bungie was real  small so it was a very intimate kind of  affair but yeah  like marathon and then myth i i was just  i was so excited about  uh this this notion of what we could do  with video games and  we would build games for the mac and and  they were going to be awesome marathons  follow-up myth  equally proved to be a success which  gave jason jones the leeway to finally  start working on his next  big project which he called monkey nuts  was it actually codenamed monkey nuts at  first  yes it was in fact codenamed monkey nuts  it went through a few iterations as it  went from stage to stage  and uh monkey nuts was one of them that  uh  yeah you could tell we were a real  mature group of young guys in our 20s  making games  in some ways if you take marathon an  epic first-person shooter and combine it  with  myths terrain-focused real-time strategy  you have the foundation for  what initially was monkey i mean uh  halo yeah our very first steps in  developing the game  initially uh the goal was to make it  very similar to myth  the sci-fi skinning of this new universe  we were creating  i remember jason and i working on  building our first  3d characters the first iteration of  this little soldier character we called  the super soldier  we began kind of flushing out the very  early foundation  of the halo universe the very early  stages of the covenant  and the forerunners at that time the  forerunners were an  actual physical entity on the  battlefield as well  one of our programmers uh charlie who  is now the president of bungie he  started programming in some physics for  the warthog how he would drive over the  cab the terrain and jump over jumps and  it got so interesting to us we were  looking at the simulation as we were  point and clicking these things around  on the map  and we're saying holy crap this looks so  interesting  why aren't we controlling them ourselves  and doing this ourselves  that would be far more fun wouldn't it  and  that's when we decided to pivot  completely and  start developing a third person action  game  the game hadn't yet been fully realized  it didn't even have an official title  but it was already being considered  max killer app and it got the attention  of a certain turtleneck wearing  executive which brings us to  the grand revealed halo at  act world we are starting to see some  great games come back to the map  but this is one of the coolest i've ever  seen halo is the name of this game  and we're going to see for the first  time halo welcome  everything you're about to see is being  rendered in real time  on a macintosh i had been working on  this game for  about a year and a half or two our team  was still small around 10 people  we were pretty nervous about showing  something off  but we were able to orchestrate a real  simple narrative and have our early  version  of the master chief running around our  early versions of the warthog  and the elites and ghosts and banshees  and all these things flying through this  beautiful open world with a colorful sky  and the rain rising up into the horizon  and even delving into the forerunner  underground facility  where there were holographic elements  flying around we were so excited about  what we 